    "## KNN imputation for filling in missing values \n",
    "\n",
    "The closing_prices DataFrame's missing values are filled in by the code using KNN imputation from the sklearn.impute module. The KNN imputer is initialized with n_neighbors=5, which means that for every missing value, it takes into account the five nearest neighbors. Since imputation depends on the similarities between rows in the same column, it uses the data from the same column the closing prices of various stocks. To ensure the integrity of the dataset, the complete dataset is subjected to the fit_transform() method, which fills in the missing values by averaging the values of the closest neighbors inside the same column. The outcome is then saved in data_cleaned where the missing values are now imputed while maintaining the original closing_prices column and index structure. The formula for **K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) Imputation** is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_{\\text{imputed}} = \\frac{1}{K} \\sum_{i=1}^{K} X_i\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_{\\text{imputed}} \\text{ is the imputed value for the missing data,}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "K \\text{ is the number of neighbors (in this case, 5 neighbors),}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_i \\text{ are the values of the nearest neighbors (those most similar to the missing value).}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The KNN imputation method uses the average of the nearest neighbors to estimate the missing value based on their similarities.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
