    "## Handling outliers with Exponential Smoothing\n",
    "\n",
    "The code implements a function called exp_smoothing that uses Pandas - ewm (Exponentially Weighted Moving) technique to apply exponential smoothing to a given columns of the data. The series (one column of data) and an optional parameter alpha, which regulates the degree of smoothing, are the two arguments that the function accepts. The smoothing will assign a 20% weight to the most recent data point and a progressively diminishing weight to earlier data points because the alpha parameter is set to 0.2 by default. This exponential smoothing function is then applied to each column (i.e., the closing price data for each stock) of the full data_cleaned DataFrame using the apply method (Reference 8).\n",
    "The formula for **Exponential Smoothing** is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_t = \\alpha \\cdot X_t + (1 - \\alpha) \\cdot S_{t-1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_t \\text{ is the smoothed value at time } t,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "X_t \\text{ is the actual value (closing price) at time } t,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\alpha \\text{ is the smoothing factor, between 0 and 1,}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "S_{t-1} \\text{ is the smoothed value at the previous time step.}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "ed3c3b12-113d-4f1e-bfe4-866b0efb17f2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def exp_smoothing(series, alpha=0.2):\n",
    "    return series.ewm(alpha=alpha).mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "data_smoothed = data_cleaned.apply(exp_smoothing)"
   ]
