   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7cc8a384-72ee-4454-9790-2f6b0f5f80ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Linear Regression Formula\n",
    "\n",
    "The formula for **Linear Regression** is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\dots + \\beta_n x_n + \\epsilon\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "y \\text{ predicted dependent variable (target),}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n \\text{ are independent variables (features),}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\beta_0 \\text{ is the intercept (constant),}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\beta_1, \\beta_2, \\dots, \\beta_n \\text{ a coefficients (weights) for the independent variables,}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\epsilon \\text{ error term (residuals).}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "91b6b95f-d085-44e6-8ebe-cb439dd06c7f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Tool for visualizing a regression model's performance is the Actual vs. Predicted values graphic. The x-axis of this figure displays the target variable's actual values, and the y-axis displays the predicted values. All points should ideally lie along a 45-degree diagonal line (seen by the red dashed line in the graphic) if the model's predictions are accurate. This would show that the predicted and actual values are equal. Points that substantially depart from this line indicate that the model's predictions are not correct in those cases.\n"
   ]
  },
