   "source": [
    "## Mean squared error\n",
    "\n",
    "The models Mean Squared Error (MSE) shows how closely the expected and actual data match; lower values imply greater performance. With the lowest MSE of 1.7429, the Gradient Boosting model appears to produce predictions that are extremely near to the actual values. With an MSE of 1.9559, Random Forest performs admirably as well, albeit somewhat better than Gradient Boosting. Linear Regression, on the other hand, has a substantially higher MSE of 22.7896, suggesting that its predictions are substantially less accurate than those of the other two models. The Generic Model's higher MSE indicates that it is not a good fit for this specific prediction task.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Formula for Mean Squared Error (MSE):\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Mean Squared Error (MSE)} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\textbf{Explanation of Formula:} \\\\\n",
    "&y_i: \\text{The observed value.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\hat{y}_i: \\text{The predicted value from the model.} \\\\\n",
    "&n: \\text{The total number of observations in the dataset.} \\\\\n",
    "&(y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2: \\text{The squared difference between the actual and predicted values for each observation.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{Squaring ensures that all errors are positive and emphasizes larger errors.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\frac{1}{n}: \\text{This divides the total sum of squared differences by the number of observations, giving the average squared error.}\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5c860fa7-1130-457e-a006-4b7e3b01a3bf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## R-squared (R²)\n",
    "\n",
    "R-squared calculates the percentage of the dependent variable's variance that the model can account for. A score nearer 1 means that a significant amount of the data variation is explained by the model. With a high R2 value of 0.9722, the Gradient Boosting model accounts for roughly 97% of the variance in the target variable. Random Forest is also quite good at identifying the underlying patterns in the data, as evidenced by its excellent R2 score of 0.9688. However, the Linear Regression Model is far less effective at modeling the data, as evidenced by its far lower R2 value of 0.6368, which shows that it only accounts for around 64% of the variance (Reference 4). \n",
    " $$\n",
    "R^2 = 1 - \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2}{\\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\bar{y})^2}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\textbf{Explanation of the Formula:} \\\\\n",
    "&y_i: \\text{The actual observed value.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\hat{y}_i: \\text{The predicted value from the model.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\bar{y}: \\text{The mean of the observed values.} \\\\\n",
    "&n: \\text{The total number of observations in the dataset.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2: \\text{The sum of squared residuals (unexplained variation).} \\\\\n",
    "&\\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\bar{y})^2: \\text{The total sum of squares (total variation).} \\\\\n",
    "&R^2: \\text{Represents the proportion of variance in the dependent variable explained by the model.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{It ranges from 0 to 1, where 1 indicates perfect prediction and 0 means no predictive power.}\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "477b54d5-5030-4f3e-b535-8b9ba926744d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## R-squared adjusted\n",
    "\n",
    "When comparing models with varying numbers of predictors, the Adjusted R-squared provides a more accurate representation of model performance by taking into account the number of features in the model. Random Forest has a little lower adjusted R2 score of 0.9657 than the Gradient Boosting model, which has 0.9695. Even after controlling for the number of predictors, these numbers show that both models perform quite well, accounting for about 97% of the variance in the data. However, the Linear Regression's adjusted R2 of just 0.6008 indicates that its explanatory power is greatly diminished when the number of predictors is taken into consideration, indicating that it is not a very good model for the data Reference 11).\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_{\\text{adj}}^2 = 1 - \\left( \\frac{(1 - R^2) \\cdot (n - 1)}{n - p - 1} \\right)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\textbf{Explanation of Formula:} \\\\\n",
    "&R^2: \\text{The standard R-squared value, representing the proportion of variance explained by the model.} \\\\\n",
    "&n: \\text{The total number of observations in the dataset.} \\\\\n",
    "&p: \\text{The number of predictors (independent variables) in the model.} \\\\\n",
    "&1 - R^2: \\text{The proportion of unexplained variance in the dependent variable.} \\\\\n",
    "&(n - 1): \\text{Degrees of freedom for the total dataset.} \\\\\n",
    "&(n - p - 1): \\text{Degrees of freedom for the residuals (after accounting for predictors).} \\\\\n",
    "&R_{\\text{adj}}^2: \\text{Adjusts \\( R^2 \\) for the number of predictors in the model. It penalizes the addition of predictors that do not significantly improve the model's performance.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{It provides a more accurate measure of model performance, especially in models with many predictors.}\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "999df9cc-0657-4c5c-b973-aaf18dac88b7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Mean absolute error\n",
    "\n",
    " Mean absolute error Better performance is shown by lower values of the Mean Absolute Error (MAE), which calculates the average magnitude of the errors in a collection of predictions. With a comparatively low MAE of 0.8651, the Gradient Boosting model's predictions are often fairly accurate. With an MAE of 0.8347, Random Forest performs similarly, demonstrating that it likewise generates predictions that are reasonably close to the actual values. The Linear Model's significantly larger MAE of 3.9472, on the other hand, indicates that its forecasts are typically far from the actual values, supporting the notion that it is an ineffective model for this task (Reference 11).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{MAE} = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left| y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\right|\n",
    "$$\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\textbf{Explanation of the Formula:} \\\\\n",
    "&y_i: \\text{The actual observed value for the \\(i^{th}\\) observation.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\hat{y}_i: \\text{The predicted value for the \\(i^{th}\\) observation.} \\\\\n",
    "&n: \\text{The total number of observations in the dataset.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\left| y_i - \\hat{y}_i \\right|: \\text{The absolute difference between the actual and predicted values for each observation.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\frac{1}{n}: \\text{Averages the absolute differences over all observations to provide the mean absolute error.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{MAE measures the average magnitude of errors in a set of predictions, without considering their direction (positive or negative).} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{It is a straightforward metric for evaluating the accuracy of regression models. Lower values indicate better model performance.}\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d34f3f20-e2b7-479b-afcd-3b2ad7901a79",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Cross-Validation Mean Score\n",
    "The Cross-Validation Mean Score provides an estimate of how well the model generalizes to unseen data by evaluating it across multiple data folds. Gradient Boosting achieves the highest cross-validation mean score of 0.9769, indicating that it generalizes well to different data subsets and is likely to perform consistently well on new, unseen data. Random Forest follows closely with a score of 0.9653, showing strong generalization ability, though not as high as Gradient Boosting. The Linear Model has a much lower cross-validation mean score of 0.6849, which indicates that it does not generalize well and performs poorly when applied to new data.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{CV Mean Score} = \\frac{1}{k} \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\text{Score}_i\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\textbf{Explanation of the Formula:} \\\\\n",
    "&k: \\text{The number of folds in cross-validation.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{Score}_i: \\text{The evaluation score (e.g., R-squared, accuracy, etc.) for the \\(i^{th}\\) fold.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\frac{1}{k}: \\text{Averages the scores across all folds to calculate the mean score.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{Cross-Validation Mean Score provides a robust estimate of model performance by splitting the data into training and validation sets multiple times.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{This helps assess how well the model generalizes to unseen data, reducing the impact of overfitting or underfitting.} \\\\\n",
    "&\\text{Higher scores indicate better model performance.}\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n"
   ]
  },
